WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.070
Film Courage: I’m wondering if we can start
out [this interview] where you were working

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.490
[before starting as a professional screenwriter],
was it at the YWCA?

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:05.490
Christine Conradt: YMCA…

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:06.490
Film Courage: YMCA…okay.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:12.820
And it sounds like you were doing fantastic
and they wanted to promote you again.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:15.870
And we can start out on that story because
it’s so fascinating?

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.050
Christine Conradt: Yes, absolutely.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:21.500
So this is a lot earlier (obviously) in my
career.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:22.900
I graduated from film school.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.230
I went to USC.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:28.529
I kind of thought (like everybody does) when
you graduate from film school that you’re

00:00:28.529 --> 00:00:33.980
going to graduate with like a three picture
deal and that really isn’t how it works.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:39.060
And so while I was in college I had worked
as a lifeguard and kind of as an aerobics

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.900
instructor at a YMCA and they kept promoting
me.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.690
And someone would quit and they would say
“Do you want to learn how to do this job.”

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:48.770
And I always said “Yes.”

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:57.020
So eventually I worked my way up to program
director and ultimately…Senior Program Director,

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.550
which is right under the Executive Director.

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.270
The Executive Director heads up a branch and
then there’s a Senior Program Director or

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.620
two and then all the program directors under
that person.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:14.900
I had been working there and I mean it was
fine, it wasn’t what my degree was in.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:21.290
At one point finally I thought well…I had
a lot of school debt.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:26.870
Going to USC obviously…I think I was about
$60,000 to $70,000 in debt at that point.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:31.590
And my executive director came to me and said
“You know, we’d really love to give you

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.130
your own branch.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:37.260
But we need a five year commitment from you
to do that.”

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:41.060
And they were going to pay me over $100,000
dollars a year and that would have been great

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.530
going toward my debt.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.570
And so I said “Let me think about that.”

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:49.950
I went home that night and I really thought
about what I wanted to do with my life.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:57.799
I had gone to film school and I really hadn’t
given it the chance that it deserved.

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:03.810
So I thought “Well I’m this much in debt
anyway, I’m going to take the chance on

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.510
being a screenwriter.”

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:11.069
I went back the next day and I thanked the
Executive Director for the offer and I said

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:17.750
“I can’t accept it, so I have to put my
2-week notice in because I need to take 6

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:21.340
months off and I’m going to take out a loan
to live on and I’m going to live my life

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.100
as a writer.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.459
And if it doesn’t work, I can come back
and I’ll let go of that dream forever.

00:02:26.459 --> 00:02:31.230
But if it does work, I owe it to myself to
give myself a chance to actually become a

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.230
screenwriter.”

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:33.780
So I did exactly that.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.790
She was not happy that I was leaving [Laughs].

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:37.790
Film Courage: Wow…

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.069
Christine Conradt: But I did put my notice
in and I did take out the loan to live on.

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:44.510
During that time I wrote 2 screenplays.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.970
I never sold either of them but they both
got me work.

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:54.020
And they got me work, and then those got me
work, and it actually ended up being really

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:56.580
the beginning of my career as a screenwriter.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.090
Film Courage: Wow!

00:02:58.090 --> 00:02:59.090
So many questions.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:00.090
Christine Conradt: I know, right?

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.323
Film Courage: And there are so many little
interesting parts to that [time in your life]

00:03:02.323 --> 00:03:04.400
and scary parts, too.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.599
And I’ve known a few people who have been
stuck with the debt [having done] something

00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:10.599
similar.

00:03:10.599 --> 00:03:12.000
So I know that is a risk.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.910
Christine Conradt: It’s a huge risk and
I would never encourage anyone to emulate

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:18.710
that in any way.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:19.710
For me, it worked.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:25.170
And I think because I had already spent the
4 years in school kind of gearing myself up

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:30.050
towards this career and I think also I needed
that time, it was such a distraction to be

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:31.050
working full-time.

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:33.910
I needed that time every day to truly be writing.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:39.590
And I spent every moment of that 6 months
furthering my career.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:44.160
Going to networking events, watching a different
movie every day and breaking it down and doing

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.520
an outline so I could understand the structure
of it, all of those types of things.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:53.130
And actually writing, spending time writing
and reading.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:59.150
For me it worked, but like I said, I’ve
known people also where it didn’t work.

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:03.140
But if you are going to do something like
that, you really have to make use of that

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:04.140
time.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:09.100
It can’t be one of those things where you’re
going to figure out what you want to do creatively

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:13.730
because I think you just end up treading water
in a way.

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:17.109
You’ve got to have a plan and you’ve got
to execute that plan.

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:18.879
Film Courage: Fascinating.

00:04:18.879 --> 00:04:23.039
I want to hear more about the 6 months, but
just one quick question or two quick questions

00:04:23.039 --> 00:04:28.169
(actually) and that is did you already know
(and forgive me if this is too personal),

00:04:28.169 --> 00:04:32.479
did you already secure this loan so you knew…or
you knew if you could get it?

00:04:32.479 --> 00:04:36.710
Christine Conradt: Trust me…when you are
$60,000 in debt (let me just explain something

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:43.089
to you), you get so many credit card offers
that you can pay $20,000, “Here’s an additional

00:04:43.089 --> 00:04:44.089
$20,000.”

00:04:44.089 --> 00:04:45.089
They all want you!

00:04:45.089 --> 00:04:46.969
You are like the best customer ever, right?

00:04:46.969 --> 00:04:51.930
Because they know you carry debt and I was
making the minimum payments.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:54.919
But again, you are going further in debt by
doing that.

00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:58.319
But you are sort of like the golden child
for the credit card companies.

00:04:58.319 --> 00:04:59.419
So I had plenty of offers.

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:03.300
I knew I could take out additional loans and
lines of credit on my credit card.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.249
Film Courage: Ah, so it wasn’t if, it was
which one?

00:05:07.249 --> 00:05:08.249
Christine Conradt: Exactly.

00:05:08.249 --> 00:05:09.249
Film Courage: Got it.

00:05:09.249 --> 00:05:13.759
And then my other question is while you were
working at the YMCA, were you also writing

00:05:13.759 --> 00:05:18.189
on the side (aside from having gone to film
school)?

00:05:18.189 --> 00:05:19.189
Christine Conradt: Yes.

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:24.559
I thought I was going to but I think at the
level where you become the Senior Program

00:05:24.559 --> 00:05:32.779
Director, I mean you are working so many hours…and
I think I had about 45 people working under

00:05:32.779 --> 00:05:33.779
me at that time.

00:05:33.779 --> 00:05:36.789
And so when they don’t show up you have
to cover [for them].

00:05:36.789 --> 00:05:42.050
So you come home and you’re not in any place
to really be creative.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:45.210
So for me (at that time) I found that I wasn’t
at that time.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.379
I had really just put all the writing on the
back burner and that was it.

00:05:48.379 --> 00:05:53.330
Even on the weekends, I would get a few hours
in, but that’s not enough to make a career

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:53.969
out of writing.

